---
import ProseLayout from '../layouts/ProseLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site.ts';
---

<ProseLayout
  title="Privacy Policy | Calcrafty"
  description="Calcrafty privacy policy: no accounts, no tracking cookies, calculator inputs that stay in your browser and cookie-free analytics that count visits anonymously."
  path="/privacy/"
  h1="Privacy Policy"
  intro="Short version: we don’t want your data, and the site is built so we never get it."
  updated="2026-11-01"
>
  <h2>Your calculator inputs stay on your device</h2>
  <p>
    Calculations run in your browser. The numbers you type are not sent to our servers. If you use a share link, your inputs are in that link’s
    address, so anyone you send it to can see them.
  </p>

  <h2>History and favorites use local storage</h2>
  <p>
    Your recent calculations (up to 10), favorite calculators and theme choice are saved in your browser’s local storage so they are there next time.
    They never leave your device. Clear them any time from the calculator page or by clearing your browser’s site data. The site works fully with
    storage turned off.
  </p>

  <h2>Analytics without cookies</h2>
  <p>
    We use privacy-friendly, cookie-free analytics to count page views and measure page speed. It does not use cookies, does not track you across
    sites, honors Do Not Track, and does not collect personal information. A sample of visits also reports anonymous page speed measurements (load
    time, layout shift and responsiveness) with the page address.
  </p>

  <h2>No accounts, no ads tracking</h2>
  <p>
    There are no accounts or sign-ups. If we show ads in the future, this policy will be updated before they appear, describing any third parties
    involved and your choices.
  </p>

  <h2>Hosting logs</h2>
  <p>
    Our web host keeps standard server logs (such as IP address, browser type and the page requested) for security and troubleshooting, for a
    limited time.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>Questions about privacy: <span>{SITE.email}</span>.</p>
</ProseLayout>
